  "title": "Building a multiplayer game world with autonomous Fable-class models",
  "content": "<p><strong><a href=\"https://twitter.com/jsnnsa/status/2115623382721126826\">How I Built the Most AI-Pilled Company in the World</a></strong></p>\nDon't be put off by the clickbait headline, this piece by Jacob Sansbury describing how they are building <a href=\"https://spawn.co\">Spawn</a> is a fascinating exploration of the bleeding edge of what's possible with agentic engineering against frontier models.</p>\n<p>Spawn is a massively multiplayer gaming environment where players can use prompts (to the in-game agent, Savi) to modify the game world.</p>\n<p>I fired it up on my phone for a few minutes and was able to prompt a castle into existence, then a dragon, then ride the dragon to fly around that castle.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Two mobile screenshots side by side from a 3D voxel-style game. Left: a first-person view from the saddle of a small teal dragon, its neck with pale diamond markings and two curved white horns in the foreground, flying toward a dark stone castle with tall teal-gray conical spires, glowing windows and teal pennants, on a rocky island in a lake with green and snowy hills behind. Top bar: profile icon, &quot;keep it&quot;, &quot;your den&quot; dropdown, &quot;+ new game&quot; button and a &quot;...&quot; menu. Right side: icon buttons and a portrait of a white-haired, freckled character labeled &quot;tap to talk&quot;. Bottom right buttons: &quot;JUMP&quot; and &quot;USE&quot;. Right: the same game with the chat panel open over the castle scene. Top bar: &quot;you",
